---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/intermediate/islam">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This intermediate lesson assumes the comparative toolkit from the foundational lesson
    <em>The World's Traditions</em> — terms such as monotheism, scripture and orthodoxy —
    and pairs naturally with <em>Studying Religion Academically</em>, the intermediate
    lesson that explains why scholars describe traditions as families of arguments rather
    than fixed codes.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>outline the life of Muhammad and the significance of the Hijra;</li>
    <li>name the Six Articles of Faith and the Five Pillars, and say what each practice enacts;</li>
    <li>explain the Sunni-Shia split as a question of succession and authority;</li>
    <li>describe how the Quran, hadith and the fiqh schools build Islamic law;</li>
    <li>use "Islam says" as the start of an argument rather than the end of one.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Islam — about 1.9 billion people, the world's second-largest and
    fastest-growing major religion — centres on <strong>submission to God
    (the literal meaning of "islam") through the Quran, the Prophet
    Muhammad's example, and the five pillars of practice</strong>. It is a
    strictly monotheistic, strongly legal, deeply textual tradition whose
    history runs from seventh-century Arabia through a golden age of
    scholarship to global diversity — with the Sunni–Shia succession split as
    its deepest internal fault line. A working rule for this page: Islam, like
    every tradition, is a family of arguments, and "Islam says" is always the
    start of one.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="origins, pillars, the succession split, and the law" widget="equationStep" config={islamCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels in order and watch
    doctrine and practice alternate: revelation, then the practice skeleton every Muslim
    shares, then the one question the sources leave open (leadership), then the legal
    method that turned sources into law. Panel 3 deserves a second pass — the split it
    describes is about succession, not about God.
  </p>

  <h2>The Prophet Muhammad</h2>
  <p>
    Muhammad ibn Abdullah (c. 570–632 CE), a merchant of Mecca, began at about
    forty — in the cave of Hira during the month of Ramadan, the night called
    <strong>Laylat al-Qadr</strong> — to receive revelations through the angel
    <strong>Jibril</strong> (Gabriel), continuing for twenty-three years. The
    message was radical in its setting: one God (against Mecca's idolatry and
    clan religion), social justice (care for orphans, the poor — themes the
    Quran presses relentlessly), and accountability before a day of judgment.
    Persecution drove the <strong>Hijra</strong> — the migration to Medina in
    622, year one of the Islamic calendar — where the Prophet built the model
    community: a constitution, a mosque as its civic centre, the umma
    (community) as a political body. By the conquest of Mecca (630) Arabia was
    largely unified under the new faith; Muhammad died in 632 as its religious
    and political head. Two frameworks matter for everything after: Muslims
    honour Muhammad as the <strong>final prophet</strong> (the "seal" of a
    prophetic line through Judaism and Christianity — Adam, Abraham, Moses,
    David, Jesus among twenty-five named in the Quran), and his
    <strong>sunnah</strong> — his reported practice — is, with the Quran, the
    model life. (Etiquette note: pious practice adds "peace be upon him" after
    his name; scholarly literature varies.)
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="order the life the paragraph tells" widget="order" config={islamProphetOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one prophet, one revealed text, and a community built at
    Medina as a model political body. The next section sets out what that community is
    asked to believe.
  </p>

  <h2>Beliefs: the Six Articles and the Shia five roots</h2>
  <p>
    Sunni theology organises belief as <strong>Six Articles of Faith</strong>:
    belief in <strong>God</strong> (Allah — Arabic for "the God", cognate with
    Syriac <em>alaha</em> and related to the Semitic root that Arabic-speaking
    Christians also use), defined above all by <strong>tawhid</strong>, the
    absolute oneness of God — shirk (associating partners with God) is the
    tradition's cardinal sin, which is why Christian trinitarian doctrine is
    (from the Islamic side) its central disagreement; in
    <strong>angels</strong> (Jibril, Mikail; record-keeping angels); in the
    <strong>revealed books</strong> (the Quran confirming — and Muslims hold,
    correcting — the Torah, Psalms and Gospel); in the <strong>prophets</strong>
    (a line culminating in Muhammad); in the <strong>Day of Judgment</strong>
    (resurrection, Paradise and Hell depicted in the Quran's vivid material
    imagery); and in <strong>divine decree</strong> (al-qadar — God's
    omniscience and will, which generated a real theological debate with human
    responsibility, the <em>qadariyya</em> dispute). Shia Islam structures the
    same faith as <strong>Five Roots of Usul ad-Din</strong>: tawhid,
    <strong>divine justice</strong> (adl — God is just in a rationally knowable
    sense, which the Sunni schools often restrain), prophethood (nubuwwah),
    and the two distinctively Shia doctrines — <strong>imamah</strong>
    (God-appointed, infallible leadership of the community after Muhammad)
    and <strong>mi'ad</strong> (the day of resurrection). Watch how much
    doctrine hangs on one word: leadership.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each article or root with its content" widget="match" config={islamArticlesMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "Allah is the name of a distinct Muslim deity, separate
  from the God of Jews and Christians." Allah is simply Arabic for "the God", cognate with
  the Semitic root the lesson names, and Arabic-speaking Christians use the same word.
  Islam presents itself as the continuation of a prophetic line through Abraham, Moses and
  Jesus — not as a new god.</p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="true or false — the name of God" widget="judge" config={islamAllahJudge} />

  <h2>Practice: the Five Pillars</h2>
  <p>
    The practice skeleton every Muslim shares is the <strong>Five Pillars</strong>.
    <strong>Shahada</strong>: the declaration — "There is no god but God, and
    Muhammad is the messenger of God" — whose sincere utterance makes one a
    Muslim. <strong>Salat</strong>: five daily prayers at fixed times (dawn,
    noon, afternoon, sunset, night), preceded by ritual ablution
    (<em>wudu</em>), performed facing Mecca (the <em>qibla</em>) — alone or
    communally, with the Friday midday congregational prayer (<em>jumu'ah</em>)
    the week's visible heartbeat. <strong>Zakat</strong>: the obligatory alms —
    classically 2.5 per cent of qualifying wealth annually — Islam's built-in
    redistribution; <em>sadaqah</em> (voluntary charity) supplements it.
    <strong>Sawm</strong>: the Ramadan fast — dawn to dusk, no food or drink —
    for the month the Quran began to descend; its last ten nights contain
    Laylat al-Qadr, and the month ends with the Id al-Fitr festival.
    <strong>Hajj</strong>: the pilgrimage to Mecca once in a lifetime for those
    able — two to three million pilgrims in the great season, dressed in plain
    white <em>ihram</em> (kings indistinguishable from the poor), circling the
    Kaaba seven times (<em>tawaf</em>), standing on the plain of Arafat — the
    rites re-enacting Abraham's family, and the gathering the largest regular
    human assembly on Earth. The pillars are famously practical theology:
    doctrine made habit, at body scale — and deliberately universal: a
    farmer in Senegal and a surgeon in Kuala Lumpur perform the same five
    actions in the same words, which is why Muslim unity is felt in the body
    before it is argued in the mind.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="call the pilgrimage" widget="estimate" config={islamHajjEst} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> belief is organised into articles, practice into five pillars
    performed in the same words worldwide. What the sources do not settle is who leads
    after the Prophet — and that omission shapes the tradition's deepest division.
  </p>

  <h2>Sunni and Shia: the succession split</h2>
  <p>
    At the Prophet's death (632) the community faced a question the Quran does
    not settle: who leads? The <strong>Sunni</strong> answer ("people of the
    sunnah and consensus"): the community's chosen caliph — <strong>Abu
    Bakr</strong>, then Umar, Uthman, Ali (the "rightly guided" four). The
    <strong>Shia</strong> answer ("the party of Ali"): leadership was
    Muhammad's — and God's — to appoint, falling to <strong>Ali</strong>, the
    Prophet's cousin and son-in-law (married to Fatima), and to his hereditary,
    divinely-guided line of <strong>imams</strong>. The dispute turned from
    politics to martyrology at <strong>Karbala</strong> (680): Ali's son
    <strong>Husayn</strong>, refusing allegiance to the Umayyad caliph, was
    killed with his small band — an event whose annual mourning
    (<em>Ashura</em>) still organises Shia identity, and whose grief-for-justice
    template runs from medieval devotions to the Iranian Revolution. The
    <strong>Twelver</strong> Shia (Iran, Iraq, Bahrain, Lebanon, Azerbaijan —
    roughly 10–15 per cent of Muslims) hold the twelfth imam went into occultation
    and returns as the Mahdi; <strong>Ismailis</strong> (following a different
    imam-line, the Aga Khan today) and <strong>Zaydis</strong> (Yemen) are other
    Shia trajectories. The majority <strong>Sunni</strong> world (roughly
    85–90 per cent) spans every school and continent — and both branches share
    Quran, Prophet, pillars and law: the split is about succession and
    authority, not about God. <strong>Sufism</strong> cuts across both: the
    mystical orders (tariqas) of inward purification — Rabia's love-mysticism,
    al-Ghazali's integration of Sufism into orthodoxy, Rumi's poetry — carried
    Islam into West Africa, South Asia and Southeast Asia.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="632: the succession question — play both answers" widget="branch" config={islamSuccessionBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the Sunni-Shia split is a theological disagreement
  about the nature of God." Both branches share the Quran, the Prophet, the pillars and
  the law; the division is about succession and authority — who may lead the community
  after Muhammad. Karbala turned a political question into a defining act of mourning.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the faulty line — the split surveyed" widget="spot-error" config={islamSplitSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the succession split turned on authority, and both branches
    still share Quran, Prophet, pillars and law. That shared law needs shared sources —
    which is where the text and its interpreters come in.
  </p>

  <h2>The Quran and its interpretation</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Quran</strong> ("recitation") is 114 <em>surahs</em> of
    revelation, compiled into one volume under the caliph <strong>Uthman</strong>
    (c. 650) from the established community copies, and transmitted with the
    same oral-superfidelity as the Vedas: millions of <em>hafiz</em> (those who
    carry the whole text by memory) make the Quran the most widely memorised
    book on Earth. Its status is sui generis: the uncreated speech of God (a
    doctrine fought over in the <em>mihna</em> inquisition of the ninth
    century), inimitable (<em>i'jaz</em> — the proof of prophethood is the
    text itself), and properly <em>in Arabic</em> — the sound matters, which
    is why the adhan is chanted and why translations are officially
    "interpretations". <strong>Interpretation</strong> is the tradition's
    other great institution: <em>tafsir</em> (commentary) explains the text
    with the Prophet's own explanations — preserved and authenticated through
    the <strong>hadith</strong> science (reports with verified chains of
    transmission, <em>isnad</em> — a historiographical apparatus without
    ancient parallel). Law then draws on four sources — Quran, sunnah,
    consensus (<em>ijma</em>) and analogy (<em>qiyas</em>) — through the
    <strong>fiqh</strong> (jurisprudence) schools: <strong>Hanafi</strong>,
    <strong>Maliki</strong>, <strong>Shafi'i</strong>, <strong>Hanbali</strong>
    among Sunnis, <strong>Ja'fari</strong> among Twelver Shia — living legal
    methods, not monoliths. <em>Sharia</em> ("the path") names God's ideal
    path; <em>fiqh</em> is the fallible human science of applying it — the
    distinction modern debates keep collapsing. The jurists' own
    deep theory, the <strong>maqasid al-sharia</strong> (the aims of the law,
    systematised by al-Ghazali and al-Shatibi), reads the law as protecting
    five goods: religion, life, intellect, lineage and property — a
    teleological frame that modernist jurists use precisely to argue for
    reform, and quietists to argue for continuity.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="order the four sources" widget="order" config={islamSourcesOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "sharia is a single, fixed book of Islamic law."
  Sharia names God's ideal path; <em>fiqh</em> is the fallible human science of applying
  it, and it runs through competing schools with different methods. Collapsing that
  distinction — as the lesson notes, modern debates keep doing — makes a living legal
  tradition look like a code.</p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drag between the path and its application" widget="compare" config={islamShariaCompare} />

  <h2>Sacred time and space</h2>
  <p>
    Islam runs on a <strong>lunar calendar</strong> (twelve months of 29–30
    days, drifting through the solar year) whose festivals are the community's
    shared clock. <strong>Id al-Fitr</strong> breaks the Ramadan fast with a
    feast and obligatory charity; <strong>Id al-Adha</strong> commemorates
    Abraham's willingness to sacrifice his son (Quran 37 — Isaac in the Jewish
    and Christian tellings, generally understood as Ishmael in Islam) with
    sacrifice and distribution of meat; <strong>Ashura</strong> is Shia
    mourning for Husayn (and a voluntary fast day for Sunnis); the month of
    Ramadan itself is the year's climax. Sacred space arranges the same
    geography: the <strong>Kaaba</strong> in Mecca's Great Mosque — the cube
    tradition traces to Abraham and Ishmael, toward which every prayer on
    Earth is oriented; <strong>Medina</strong>'s Prophet's Mosque; <strong>Jerusalem</strong>
    (the third holy city — the direction of prayer before Mecca, site of
    Muhammad's night journey, the Al-Aqsa compound). The <strong>mosque</strong>
    (masjid, "place of prostration") is the local unit: the <em>mihrab</em>
    niche marking the qibla, the <em>minbar</em> pulpit for the Friday sermon,
    the minaret for the call to prayer — and, characteristically, no altar and
    no clergy of sacramental rank: the imam leads prayer; scholars
    (<em>ulama</em>) carry interpretive authority, which is a different kind
    of hierarchy and the reason "who speaks for Islam?" has no Vatican-style
    answer.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="pair each time or place with its role" widget="match" config={islamSacredMatch} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What makes the Hijra (622 CE) a hinge in Islamic history rather than just a journey?</li>
    <li>Which pillar is performed five times daily, and which once in a lifetime for those able?</li>
    <li>The lesson says "Islam says" is always the start of an argument. Give one example from this page where a single term covers competing positions.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) It begins the Islamic calendar and marks the founding of
    the model community at Medina — a constitution, a mosque as civic centre, the umma as a
    political body. (2) Salat, the five daily prayers; hajj, the pilgrimage to Mecca.
    (3) Strong answers include sharia versus fiqh (ideal path versus fallible human
    schools), the "seal of prophethood" in the Ahmadiyya dispute, or imamah — the Shia
    doctrine of appointed leadership that Sunnis do not hold.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Islam is a strictly monotheistic, textual tradition: Quran, the Prophet's sunnah, and five pillars of practice.</li>
    <li>Tawhid — the absolute oneness of God — is the core doctrine; shirk is the cardinal sin.</li>
    <li>The Five Pillars are doctrine made habit, performed in the same words worldwide.</li>
    <li>The Sunni-Shia split is about succession and authority, not about God.</li>
    <li>Law draws on four sources through the fiqh schools; sharia is the ideal path, fiqh the fallible application.</li>
    <li>Hadith chains (isnad) are a historiographical apparatus without ancient parallel.</li>
    <li>Sufism cuts across both branches and carried Islam into West, South and Southeast Asia.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling intermediate lessons <em>Judaism</em>, <em>Christianity</em>,
    <em>Hinduism</em> and <em>Buddhism</em> can now be read comparatively, asking the same
    questions about succession, law and interpretation; the advanced lesson <em>Scripture
    and Interpretation</em> returns to the tafsir and isnad machinery in depth.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four extensions. <strong>The golden age</strong> (8th–13th centuries):
    Abbasid Baghdad's translation movement and House of Wisdom absorbed Greek,
    Persian and Indian learning — al-Khwarizmi's algebra (his name became
    "algorithm"), Avicenna's medical canon (Europe's textbook for centuries),
    Averroes' Aristotle — while theologians argued the Greek inheritance's
    limits (al-Ghazali's <em>Incoherence of the Philosophers</em>; the
    European scholastics then argued with Averroes). This is the "intellectual
    golden age" the summary names, and the history lesson gives it the full
    treatment. <strong>Art and aniconism</strong>: the hadith traditions
    discouraging figural imagery produced the civilization-defining
    alternatives — calligraphy, geometric pattern, arabesque — though figural
    art flourished too (Persian miniatures, Ottoman painting): the "no images"
    rule is jurisprudentially contested, as everything here is. Women's
    history is a similar recovery-in-progress: Khadija was a merchant who
    employed and then married the Prophet; Aisha transmitted a large share of
    hadith; and the modern debates over women's scholarship, prayer leadership
    and family law are, on one accurate reading, an argument about the
    tradition's own first century. <strong>Modern
    currents are plural</strong>: Islamic modernism (Muhammad Abduh: the
    tradition can absorb the scientific worldview), Salafism (return to the
    first generations — quietist, political and jihadi variants),
    Islamism (state-building political Islam), and secular Muslim life — a
    1,400-year tradition read through every modern political vocabulary.
    <strong>The finality dispute</strong>: the Ahmadiyya movement (19th-century
    India) holds Mirza Ghulam Ahmad a prophet-subordinate-to-Muhammad —
    which most Muslims hold violates the seal of prophethood, and Pakistan
    constitutionally declares Ahmadis non-Muslim (1984): doctrine, politics
    and identity braided in one word, "khatm". The open questions run from
    Quranic authorship and the early manuscript record (the Sanaa palimpsest,
    radiocarbon-dated to the seventh century) to gender and law across the
    Muslim world — arguments the tradition is visibly having with itself, as
    it has for fourteen centuries.
  </p>
